WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.670
A capital, a number, a symbol…

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.400
my name, an at sign and my birth year.

3
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.540
Same as my email and mobile money.

4
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:11.700
Strong!

5
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.020
Today: why long beats complicated

6
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.120
— and how to make passwords you

7
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.220
can actually remember.

8
00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.110
Criminals don’t guess passwords by hand.

9
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.920
Programs try millions of guesses — names,

10
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.120
birthdays, football teams — and they know

11
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.060
our tricks: a capital first, an at sign

12
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.220
for “a”, a year at the end.

13
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.580
That password is an easy guess.

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.020
What really slows a program down

15
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.980
is length and surprise.

16
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.130
A padlock with three dials has

17
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.230
a thousand combinations.

18
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.500
Six dials?

19
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:50.570
A million.

20
00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.450
Every extra character multiplies the work.

21
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.800
So here’s the idea: a passphrase.

22
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.880
Pick three random words — words

23
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.820
that have nothing to do with you or

24
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.600
each other — and join them.

25
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.230
Long, hard to guess and easy to picture.

26
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.780
That’s the advice of the UK’s

27
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.540
National Cyber Security Centre.

28
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.020
Kettle, mango, thunder.

29
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.020
Picture a kettle full of mangoes in a

30
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.300
thunderstorm — you won’t forget it.

31
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.640
Don’t use ours, though: make your own.

32
00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:27.430
Quick check!

33
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.460
Which would a guessing program crack

34
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.880
first: a name with a year and a

35
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.620
symbol, or three random words?

36
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.480
The name, year and symbol —

37
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.700
it’s short and predictable.

38
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.950
Three random words are longer

39
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.790
and much harder to guess.

40
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.520
One password for everything is risky too.

41
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.850
If one website leaks it,

42
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:55.670
criminals try it everywhere.

43
00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:59.610
Email matters most — reset links go there.

44
00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.370
So give email and mobile money their own.

45
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.040
Too many to remember?

46
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.990
A written list, kept safe at home,

47
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.210
beats reusing one password.

48
00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:14.450
And some things you never share: your

49
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:18.370
password, your PIN, or a one-time code —

50
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.570
the short code sent to your phone.

51
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.910
No real bank or mobile money company

52
00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.910
will ask you to tell them these.

53
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.300
Two old rules you can let go: forcing

54
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.240
symbols, and changing passwords every

55
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:32.480
month.

56
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.070
NIST, the US standards agency, tells

57
00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:37.930
organisations to allow long

58
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.730
passwords and ask for a change only

59
00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.190
if one may have been stolen.

60
00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:43.970
Your turn.

61
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.720
A caller says he’s from my bank and

62
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.500
wants the code just sent to my phone.

63
00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:49.930
What should I do?

64
00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.130
Hang up and call the bank yourself.

65
00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.070
Remember: three random words, a different

66
00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.250
passphrase for email and money, and never

67
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.490
share a password, PIN or code.

68
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.740
Try it in class tomorrow: put

69
00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.100
word cards in a bag.

70
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.710
Pupils pull out three, draw one funny

71
00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.810
picture that links them, and see how easy

72
00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.950
a practice passphrase is to remember.
